    en: {
      title: 'Gas Sayago: US$ 213 million and not one cubic metre of gas',
      dek: 'A PwC audit commissioned by UTE concluded the project was never viable; prosecutors shelved the criminal complaint and months later the case was reopened.',
      contexto: 'Gas Sayago S.A. was set up in 2012 as a state-owned corporation — 79% UTE, 21% ANCAP — to build a regasification terminal at Puntas de Sayago, in Montevideo bay. The works were contracted to the GNLS consortium and civil construction went to Brazil\'s OAS; the aim was to supply liquefied natural gas to Uruguay and export to Argentina. In 2015 the consortium abandoned the project, the contract was terminated for breach and the State collected a US$ 100 million guarantee. The terminal never gasified a single cubic metre: what remains are unfinished works and the piles driven into the Río de la Plata. The company was later placed in liquidation.',
      hallazgo: 'In June 2021 UTE\'s board released the management audit it had commissioned from PricewaterhouseCoopers. According to the information circulated by the Presidency and by Subrayado, the venture had produced a US$ 213 million loss to that date: US$ 113 million contributed by UTE and ANCAP and US$ 100 million the State had collected in 2015 by calling in the guarantee. According to the Presidency, the audit found no business plan drawn up by UTE or ANCAP and no financial feasibility model for the project, and recorded spending of US$ 290,000 on foreign travel, US$ 247,000 on training and US$ 212,000 on catering. Subrayado also reported some US$ 10 million in pay between 2012 and 2019 and about US$ 8 million to dismantle the piles. In May 2021 El Observador had published a breakdown including some US$ 150,000 in foreign travel allowances, US$ 70,000 on master\'s and postgraduate degrees, US$ 200,000 on lunches, dinners and catering and US$ 20,000 on workplace gymnastics and massages, based on data from Enrique Pées Boz, then a UTE director for Cabildo Abierto, who called the project «failed wishful thinking, scandalously expensive». In 2023 UTE president Silvia Emaldi updated the total cost before the Senate Finance Committee to US$ 256 million, of which US$ 156 million falls on the State.',
      statusNote: 'Organised Crime prosecutor Luis Pacheco asked for the criminal complaint to be shelved in mid-2023: according to la diaria, he found no evidence confirming the alleged irregularities. In August 2023 Parliament unanimously approved the liquidation of the company and the transfer of its assets and liabilities to UTE and ANCAP. In October 2023 the case was reopened at the request of ANCAP\'s lawyer, Gustavo Bordes: prosecutors said they would seek further testimony to try to establish possible interference by Brazilian government officials in the hiring of the builder OAS, and noted the case had been shelved «without prejudice», meaning it could be reopened if new facts emerged. As of this file\'s closing date (August 2026), no developments later than October 2023 are on record. No one has been convicted in this case.',
      porQueImporta: 'The US$ 156 million that UTE says was left to the State came out of UTE\'s and ANCAP\'s balance sheets — the same pocket that pays the electricity bill and the litre of petrol. Add the liquidation costs and the removal of the piles left in the bay. Not one household received a cubic metre of gas in return.',
      caveat: 'Gas Sayago was a corporation: its purchases were not published in the state procurement system, so the case leaves almost no trace in the contract search. What can appear are UTE and ANCAP purchases linked to the project.',
    },
