      contexto: 'Raúl Sendic chaired ANCAP and was Vice-President of Uruguay until 2017, when he resigned amid the scandal over the company\'s corporate cards and an academic degree he did not hold. The criminal case had two strands: brokering with the finance house Exor over an ANCAP debt with Venezuela\'s PDVSA, and personal purchases made with the company\'s corporate card.',
      hallazgo: 'On 20 May 2021, as Subrayado reported, judge María Helena Mainard sentenced Sendic to 18 months in prison, four years\' disqualification from public office and a fine of 500 UR (about $ 700,000 at the time), with the sentence conditionally suspended as he had no prior record: the conviction would be extinguished if he committed no new offence within a year. The ruling held that he exceeded his powers in the Exor dealings over the PDVSA debt, and that there were corporate-card purchases unrelated to his duties. The defence appealed the following day. Earlier, in February 2020, the Supreme Court had dismissed prosecutor Luis Pacheco\'s cassation appeal and removed four former ANCAP executives from the case — Germán Riet, José Coya, Juan Gómez and Juan Amaro — leaving Sendic as the sole defendant.',
