      dek: 'ANCAP halted the country\'s only refinery from September 2023 to April 2024 for US$ 79 million of maintenance, and had to import finished fuel throughout.',
      contexto: 'The La Teja refinery is Uruguay\'s only one. In 2023 ANCAP decided on a full plant shutdown to upgrade the catalytic cracker. The refinery stopped in September 2023 and resumed in April 2024. Throughout that period the country was supplied with imported product: the company\'s president at the time, Alejandro Stipanicic, explained the idea was to make large imports very few times, using vessels of 80,000 to 100,000 cubic metres.',
      hallazgo: 'In April 2023 Ámbito reported that halting production during the four months of maintenance would cost ANCAP some US$ 180 million, that 600,000 cubic metres of product would be imported and that equipment and maintenance costs were around US$ 77 million. In November 2023 the Presidency reported the investment was US$ 79 million: US$ 67 million on the catalytic cracker upgrade — including workers\' contracts — US$ 5 million on imported materials and equipment and US$ 7 million on overtime, with some 470 ANCAP staff and about 1,000 outside workers on site. In July 2023 El Observador had warned that the real duration was uncertain — three to four months on two shifts, or up to nine if the Fancap union, in dispute over bringing private partners into the cement business, demanded an eight-hour Monday-to-Friday shift — and that ANCAP could have to pay penalties for each day of delay on commitments made with around 16 contractor firms.',
